me c3? Not possible due to primary key. So DISTINCT in Q2 is redundant. They are equivalent. However, consider if table2 has a row with c3 that does not exist in table1? Foreign key constraint ensures c3 in table2 references table1.c3, so all c3 in table2 exist in table1. So join will always find a match. Thus, they are equivalent. But wait, Q1's subquery selects c3 from table2 where MIN and MAX conditions hold, then joins with table1 on c3. If table2 has a c3 that satisfies condition but table1 does not have that c3? Impossible due to foreign key. So they are equivalent. Unless there is a misinterpretation: Q2 groups by S.c3, but S is alias for table2 in join. So it groups by table2.c3. After joining, for each group, it selects P.c3, P.c4. But P.c3 is table1.c3, which equals S.c3 due to join condition. So same. I think they are equivalent. But the task is to find counterexample, so perhaps I missed something. Let's think of edge case: What if table2 has no rows for a given c3? Then Q1 subquery won't include that c3, so join won't include it. Q2: JOIN table2 AS S ON P.c3 = S.c3 will not match, so no rows in result. So both exclude. What if table2 has rows for c3 but all have c6 NULL? Then MIN(c6) and MAX(c6) are NULL, and comparison with date yields UNKNOWN, so HAVING filters out. Both exclude. So they are equivalent. But perhaps the difference is in handling of multiple rows in table1? No. Wait, Q2 has GROUP BY S.c3, but what if table1 has two rows with different c3 that join to same S.c3? Not possible because join is on c3, and c3 is primary key in table1. So each S.c3 joins to exactly one P.c3. Thus, they are equivalent. I conclude they are equivalent, but the problem implies they are not. Let's re-read Q2: 'GROUP BY S.c3' – this groups by table2.c3. But the SELECT clause has P.c3, P.c4. In standard SQL, if you group by S.c3, you can select P.c3 only if it is functionally dependent on S.c3. Since join condition is P.c3 = S.c3, P.c3 equals S.c3 for each row, so it