ey are equivalent. But wait, what if all rows have c6 NULL? Then Q1: MIN(c6) and MAX(c6) are NULL, HAVING condition NULL, exclude. Q2: SUM(within) = 0, exclude. Same. So I conclude they are equivalent. However, the task might expect a counterexample based on a misinterpretation. Perhaps the intended difference is that Q1's subquery selects distinct c3 from table2 that satisfy the condition, then joins with table1. Q2 joins first, then groups. If table2 has rows with same c3 but different c6, and some within some outside, both exclude. But what if table2 has a row with c6 exactly '2019-01-01' and another with c6 exactly '2019-03-31'? Both within, so include. So no. I'll try to construct a case where Q1 includes but Q2 excludes. That would require MIN and MAX within range but Q2's sum conditions not met. For Q2 to exclude, either SUM(within) = 0 or SUM(outside) > 0. If MIN and MAX within range, then all rows are within range, so SUM(outside)=0 and SUM(within) = count >0. So Q2 includes. So Q1 include implies Q2 include. Conversely, Q2 include implies all rows within range, so MIN and MAX within range, so Q1 include. So they are equivalent. Therefore, no counterexample exists. But the task says 'generate a counterexample', so perhaps I must provide one even if they are equivalent? Or maybe I made a mistake. Let's consider NULL in c6: if c6 is NULL for some rows, MIN and MAX ignore NULL, so if non-NULL rows are within range, Q1 includes. Q2: SUM(within) counts non-NULL within rows, SUM(outside) counts non-NULL outside rows. NULL rows are ignored in both sums. So if there are NULL rows and non-NULL within rows, both include. If all rows are NULL, both exclude. So same. What if there is a row with c6 outside range and a row with NULL? Q1: MIN or MAX will be outside (since non-NULL outside), exclude. Q2: SUM(outside) >0, exclude. Same. So indeed equivalent. I think the only possible difference is if the boolean expression in SUM is misinterpreted. For example, in some SQL,