    "explanation": "Q1 filters rows before grouping using WHERE c1 BETWEEN '2019-06-28' AND '2019-07-27', while Q2 filters after grouping using HAVING DATEDIFF('2019-07-27', c1) BETWEEN 0 AND 29. The DATEDIFF function calculates the difference in days between '2019-07-27' and c1, so DATEDIFF('2019-07-27', c1) BETWEEN 0 AND 29 means c1 must be between '2019-06-28' and '2019-07-27' inclusive. However, the key difference is that Q2's HAVING clause filters grouped results, which means days outside the date range that have no rows (zero active users) will be excluded from Q2's output, while Q1's WHERE clause excludes those rows before grouping, so days with zero rows won't appear in either query. The real divergence occurs with NULL dates: if c1 is NULL, Q1's WHERE clause excludes those rows (NULL BETWEEN anything is NULL/false), so they don't affect grouping. But Q2 groups NULL dates together (since GROUP BY NULL puts all NULLs in one group), then the HAVING clause evaluates DATEDIFF('2019-07-27', NULL), which returns NULL, and NULL BETWEEN 0 AND 29 is NULL/false, so the NULL group is filtered out. However, since the schema doesn't specify NOT NULL for c1, NULL dates are allowed, creating a counterexample.",
