r is essentially counting 1 for each group that has min_date+1 present. Q2: numerator is COUNT(DISTINCT A1.c4) where A1.c4 = A2.c4 AND A1.c2+1 = A2.c2 AND (A1.c4, A1.c2) is the min date pair. This also counts 1 for each group that has min_date+1 present. They seem equivalent. But wait, Q2 has a cross join between table1 AS A1 and table1 AS A2 (implicit CROSS JOIN due to comma), with conditions A1.c4 = A2.c4 AND A1.c2+1 = A2.c2. This finds all consecutive pairs within same group. Then it filters to keep only those where A1 is the minimum date for its group. So if a group has min_date=M and also has date M+1, it will be counted. If a group has min_date=M and has date M+2 but not M+1, it won't be counted because A1.c2+1 must equal A2.c2. So still same as Q1. Maybe the difference is in handling of groups with only one row? For group with single row date D, Q1: TEMP has min_date=D, join with A gives one row where A.c2=D, so CASE returns 0 (since D+1 ≠ D), numerator contribution 0. Q2: No row satisfies A1.c2+1 = A2.c2 because only one row, so numerator contribution 0. Same. What about groups with duplicate dates? Primary key (c4, c2) prevents duplicate dates per group. So no duplicates. Let's consider NULLs? c2 is DATE, part of PK, so NOT NULL. c4 is INT, part of PK, so NOT NULL. No NULLs in join columns. Maybe the difference is in the denominator? Q1 denominator: COUNT(DISTINCT TEMP.c4) which is number of groups. Q2 denominator: (SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT c4) FROM table1) which is also number of groups. Same. Wait, Q1's FROM clause: (SELECT c4, MIN(c2) AS MIN_DATE FROM table1 GROUP BY c4) AS TEMP JOIN table1 AS A ON TEMP.c4 = A.c4. This is an INNER JOIN. For groups with at least one row, TEMP will have one row, and join with A will produce at least one row. For empty table? Not relevant. Q2's FROM: table1 AS A1, table1 AS A2 with conditions. This is also an inner join. Both handle empty table same. I need to find a difference. Let's construct a case where a group has min_dat